Z||000000||87GT||06-03-99||06:00 PM||||Hello.  Just recently, I decided to find out how many 87 Fiero GTs were made with a 5-Sp. I went to Barnes and Noble the other day and purchased a Pontiac history book of all models that Pontiac made from 26' to 95'(It was wrapped in plastic).  To make a long story short, it listed all the production numbers for the 84-86 fieros, but when I turned to the page with 87 Fieros, it said the dreaded.. N/A!  Does anybody know the production numbers of the 87 Fiero GT's?  In particular, ones with 5-SP's?  Thanks.  
Z||000001||RossT||06-03-99||10:45 PM||||46,581 Fiero's were made in 1987<p>23,603 sprt cp L4<br>  3,875 se L4<br>  3,135 se V6<br>15,966 gt V6<br>         2 experim'tl<p>I have seen a back issue of FOCA (1998 i <br>think) where the information is further broken down to 5-speed, sunroof etc.... I no longer have that issue.  But I am sure<br>that someone out there does...<p>I to just bought my first Fiero. Its a long long<br>long story. But to sum it up I bought an 88gt with 475 miles on it, formely owned by<br>by a pontiac executive.  Its a blast!!!<p>RossT.<br>
Z||000002||87GT||06-04-99||08:18 PM||||Hey RossT, thanks for the info!  It is greatly appreciated.  To find a 88' GT with that kind of mileage, must have cost you a pretty penny. Have fun!  see ya.
Z||000003||RossT||06-08-99||12:13 AM||||87gt,  Yes it did cost alot. But I have learned from other  cars that I have owned in the past (mopars), that with only a few exceptions,  you are  always better off spending the big bucks up front for a nice car, rather than trying to fix up an average car. After many months of searching I concluded that a collector show car will cost you about $10,000 US.<br>I found many such cars for sale and they all had around 10-20 K miles showing. My 88GT 475 mile fiero cost $12,900.  To me, it was well worth it. Thank god for that home equity loan!! <p>Ross.
